1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,055
If your ankles roll in on inline speed skates, this guide shows you how to find the real cause and fix it.

2
00:00:07,055 --> 00:00:14,111
You will learn a two minute test you can do at home, the four usual causes, and the ankle work that helps.

3
00:00:14,111 --> 00:00:15,575
Here is the good news.

4
00:00:15,575 --> 00:00:18,038
The cause is usually not weak ankles.

5
00:00:18,038 --> 00:00:20,634
It is almost always one of four things.

6
00:00:20,634 --> 00:00:23,230
Your weight sits inside the wheel line.

7
00:00:23,230 --> 00:00:25,560
Your wheels have worn into a slope.

8
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:28,089
Your frame is mounted off to one side.

9
00:00:28,089 --> 00:00:30,952
Or your boot is not holding your heel down.

10
00:00:30,952 --> 00:00:34,546
Three of those change the same afternoon you fix them.

11
00:00:35,246 --> 00:00:38,032
First, be clear about what you are fixing.

12
00:00:38,032 --> 00:00:42,477
Every stroke in speed skating puts the skate on an edge on purpose.

13
00:00:42,477 --> 00:00:43,870
That is not collapse.

14
00:00:43,870 --> 00:00:50,902
Collapse is when the skate tips inward while you are simply gliding, with no push, and you cannot stop it.

15
00:00:50,902 --> 00:00:52,295
Look for these signs.

16
00:00:52,295 --> 00:00:55,878
The skate tips in during the glide, not only the push.

17
00:00:55,878 --> 00:00:59,261
One skate's wheels are more coned than the other's.

18
00:00:59,261 --> 00:01:03,639
Your arch aches after a session while your quads feel barely used.

19
00:01:03,639 --> 00:01:08,018
Standing still, your kneecap sits inside the line of your big toe.

20
00:01:08,018 --> 00:01:12,330
If the skate only tips when you push hard, you are probably fine.

21
00:01:12,330 --> 00:01:13,856
Edge work is the sport.

22
00:01:14,556 --> 00:01:20,077
Before you buy anything or start any exercises, do this test on a flat floor at home.

23
00:01:20,077 --> 00:01:24,169
Put your skates on and lace them exactly as you normally would.

24
00:01:24,169 --> 00:01:30,080
Hold a wall lightly and note where your kneecap sits compared with the middle of your foot.

25
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:34,497
Now let go and stand on one skate for a few seconds, then the other.

26
00:01:34,497 --> 00:01:36,510
Watch the frame, not your knee.

27
00:01:36,510 --> 00:01:40,148
Next, loosen the laces from the ankle upward and repeat.

28
00:01:40,148 --> 00:01:43,266
If the roll improves, the closure is forcing it.

29
00:01:43,266 --> 00:01:48,072
Finally, step out of the skates and do the same single leg stand barefoot.

30
00:01:48,072 --> 00:01:53,204
If your ankle rolls in barefoot too, your foot and ankle are part of the story.

31
00:01:53,204 --> 00:02:00,089
If your barefoot stance is quiet, the gear is doing it to you, and no amount of calf raises will fix that.

32
00:02:00,789 --> 00:02:03,938
This diagram shows the difference from the front.

33
00:02:03,938 --> 00:02:06,316
On the left, the ankle has rolled in.

34
00:02:06,316 --> 00:02:11,008
The shin stays upright, but the boot and frame fold inward underneath it.

35
00:02:11,008 --> 00:02:19,043
The orange line shows the load landing to the inside of the contact patch, the only narrow strip of road you are standing on.

36
00:02:19,043 --> 00:02:21,292
On the right, the ankle is stacked.

37
00:02:21,292 --> 00:02:27,334
Shin, boot and wheel sit in one straight column, so the load lands right on the contact patch.

38
00:02:27,334 --> 00:02:30,484
In a proper lean, the whole column tips together.

39
00:02:30,484 --> 00:02:33,248
In a collapse, only the bottom of it folds.

40
00:02:33,948 --> 00:02:40,614
The first cause is the most common by a long way, and it is a technique problem, not a gear problem.

41
00:02:40,614 --> 00:02:45,947
A single line of wheels gives you a contact strip only a few millimetres across.

42
00:02:45,947 --> 00:02:49,014
Your foot has to be parked right on top of it.

43
00:02:49,014 --> 00:02:57,347
When your hips sit even slightly to the inside, your ankle is left holding a sideways load it has almost no leverage against.

44
00:02:57,347 --> 00:02:59,747
It tires quickly and starts to fold.

45
00:02:59,747 --> 00:03:01,280
The giveaway is timing.

46
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:09,546
The roll is worst at low speed and during long glides, and it almost disappears when you skate hard and change feet quickly.

47
00:03:09,546 --> 00:03:12,813
The correction belongs to the hip, not the ankle.

48
00:03:12,813 --> 00:03:18,213
You build it by holding low glides on one skate for far longer than feels useful.

49
00:03:18,913 --> 00:03:21,004
The second cause is easy to miss.

50
00:03:21,004 --> 00:03:22,906
Speed wheels do not wear flat.

51
00:03:22,906 --> 00:03:28,166
They wear from the shoulder you load, which for most skaters is the inner shoulder.

52
00:03:28,166 --> 00:03:31,335
As that shoulder goes, the crown moves off centre.

53
00:03:31,335 --> 00:03:40,145
The wheel stops offering a high point in the middle and starts offering a ramp, so your skate sits at a small angle every time you load it.

54
00:03:40,145 --> 00:03:44,645
Take a wheel off, hold it at eye level, and look at the profile end on.

55
00:03:44,645 --> 00:03:49,461
If the two shoulders are not mirror images, that wheel is tilting your foot.

56
00:03:49,461 --> 00:03:53,835
When you rotate, flip each wheel over and move it to the other skate.

57
00:03:53,835 --> 00:03:58,144
And if a set has coned badly, it is finished for stability training.

58
00:03:58,144 --> 00:04:01,630
Replacing all eight at once gives you matched profiles.

59
00:04:02,330 --> 00:04:04,517
The third cause is frame position.

60
00:04:04,517 --> 00:04:09,597
Your wheel line wants to sit directly under the point your foot actually loads.

61
00:04:09,597 --> 00:04:13,712
In this diagram, the orange dot is where your foot presses down.

62
00:04:13,712 --> 00:04:20,400
On the left, the frame is mounted too far to the outside, so the wheel line sits outboard of that point.

63
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:30,432
Every bit of load you put through the skate becomes a small turning force that rolls the boot inward, for the whole session, with no technique fault at all.

64
00:04:30,432 --> 00:04:36,477
On the right, the frame sits right under the load, and the wheel line and the load line agree.

65
00:04:37,177 --> 00:04:45,265
Most speed boots allow a few millimetres of sideways adjustment at the mounting points, so this usually costs nothing.

66
00:04:45,265 --> 00:04:51,091
Mark the current position on the boot sole with a pencil before you loosen the bolts.

67
00:04:51,091 --> 00:04:56,849
Then move the frame toward the inside of the foot in steps of about two millimetres.

68
00:04:56,849 --> 00:05:04,458
If your arch starts complaining, go back a step, because a frame too far inside loads the arch on every stroke.

69
00:05:04,458 --> 00:05:06,103
Ride height matters too.

70
00:05:06,103 --> 00:05:15,082
A taller wheel lifts you further off the road, so the same small tilt at the ankle carries your weight further from the wheel line.

71
00:05:15,082 --> 00:05:24,815
If you are fighting stability on 110mm wheels, dropping to a 90mm or 100mm setup for a block of training is a smart move, not a step backward.

72
00:05:25,515 --> 00:05:27,377
The fourth cause is the boot.

73
00:05:27,377 --> 00:05:35,720
If your heel lifts inside it, your foot slides a few millimetres on every stroke and lands somewhere slightly different each time.

74
00:05:35,720 --> 00:05:40,918
Your ankle spends the whole session hunting for a position it never gets to keep.

75
00:05:40,918 --> 00:05:43,293
Most skaters lace this the wrong way.

76
00:05:43,293 --> 00:05:46,566
They pull the top of the cuff as tight as they can.

77
00:05:46,566 --> 00:05:52,662
That restricts the forward ankle flex you need to get low, and it leaves the heel just as free.

78
00:05:52,662 --> 00:05:56,577
Instead, pull the laces hard over the instep, above the arch.

79
00:05:56,577 --> 00:06:00,556
That is the band that drives your heel back into the heel cup.

80
00:06:00,556 --> 00:06:02,546
Above the ankle bone, ease off.

81
00:06:02,546 --> 00:06:07,038
A cuff you can still fold forward with your hand will let you get low.

82
00:06:07,738 --> 00:06:12,512
If the barefoot test showed a roll, strength work belongs in your week.

83
00:06:12,512 --> 00:06:15,066
Gear faults change the same afternoon.

84
00:06:15,066 --> 00:06:19,638
Strength changes over six to eight weeks of consistent, boring work.

85
00:06:19,638 --> 00:06:21,991
Ten minutes on most days is plenty.

86
00:06:21,991 --> 00:06:24,008
Start with resisted inversion.

87
00:06:24,008 --> 00:06:29,252
Anchor a band on the outside of your foot and turn the sole inward against it.

88
00:06:29,252 --> 00:06:32,412
This trains the muscle that holds your arch up.

89
00:06:32,412 --> 00:06:33,958
Next, short foot holds.

90
00:06:33,958 --> 00:06:42,228
Draw the ball of your foot gently back toward the heel so the arch domes, keep your toes relaxed, and hold for ten seconds.

91
00:06:42,228 --> 00:06:44,715
Add raises for the front of the shin.

92
00:06:44,715 --> 00:06:51,640
Then slow calf raises through the full range, two legs first, then one leg, controlled on the way down.

93
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:53,455
None of it needs equipment.

94
00:06:53,455 --> 00:06:55,540
Consistency is the whole trick.

95
00:06:56,239 --> 00:07:00,815
Everything in this guide is general training advice, not a diagnosis.

96
00:07:00,815 --> 00:07:14,740
See a physiotherapist or a doctor rather than working through it yourself if you have sharp pain in or around the ankle, visible swelling, numbness or tingling in the foot, or an ankle that gives way under you.

97
00:07:14,740 --> 00:07:20,177
The same goes for any pain that lasts more than a few days after you stop skating.

98
00:07:20,177 --> 00:07:25,615
And if your ankle has been sprained before, get professional advice on loading it.

99
00:07:25,615 --> 00:07:33,108
The ligaments and balance reflexes both need specific work after an injury, and a general list will not cover it.

100
00:07:33,807 --> 00:07:35,504
So here is where to start.

101
00:07:35,504 --> 00:07:38,113
Do the two minute standing test tonight.

102
00:07:38,113 --> 00:07:45,354
If the roll disappears barefoot, check your wheel profiles and your frame position before you look at yourself.

103
00:07:45,354 --> 00:07:52,856
If it is there barefoot as well, start the ankle work and give it two months, while watching for the warning signs.

104
00:07:52,856 --> 00:07:56,965
Either way, hold more single leg glides than feels comfortable.

105
00:07:56,965 --> 00:08:02,771
A skater who can stand quietly on one skate at low speed has already solved most of this.

106
00:08:02,771 --> 00:08:08,447
The full guide, with the answers on ankle support and insoles, is on the LiveLica blog.

107
00:08:08,447 --> 00:08:10,469
The link is in the description.
